reports/historical_source_screen_round2.json:11:      "versioned_id": "1811.10861v1", "as_of": "2018-12-31T23:59:59Z", "source_pdf_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1811.10861v1", "source_pdf_sha256": "1693a0a1d630a12dbb5f1b540aafb8db7394f2f4b3b73cd07f87b5a240011dd3", "source_status": "section_screened", "question_status": "eligible_source_open", "source_locator": "PDF p. 11, §6 Summary", "source_basis": "AstroServ reports an implemented ingestion/query system, then explicitly proposes a future polystore framework that automatically adjusts distribution of data across storage systems to improve performance. That automatic redistribution was not evaluated here.", "candidate_question": "Under the same astronomical streaming and historical-query workload, can automatic cross-store data redistribution improve query latency over a fixed layout while meeting the original ingestion-latency target?", "competing_explanations": "Adaptation improves placement; or migration overhead and workload drift eliminate any gain.", "candidate_falsification": "On frozen independent workloads and equal resources, no query-latency advantage or violation of the ingestion target fails the joint benefit claim.", "future_outcome_status": "sealed_not_searched"
reports/historical_source_screen_round2.json:14:      "versioned_id": "1905.04752v1", "as_of": "2019-12-31T23:59:59Z", "source_pdf_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1905.04752v1", "source_pdf_sha256": "2e13c14dccf9190d3d0c09ab21e016146a2433b952b130a0fceaa26730a8f776", "source_status": "section_screened", "question_status": "eligible_source_open", "source_locator": "PDF p. 6, §IV.A Memory Leaks", "source_basis": "This overview identifies memory-leak detection as a limitation of syntactic/pattern-based Scala static analysis and argues that semantic information is needed. It does not implement or evaluate such a detector.", "candidate_question": "At matched review budget on independently confirmed Scala memory leaks, does a semantic-aware static detector find more true leaks than syntax/pattern-only analysis without an unacceptable false-positive increase?", "competing_explanations": "Semantics resolves hidden leaks; or analysis complexity and false positives offset recall gains.", "candidate_falsification": "No independently verified recall gain at a prespecified false-positive ceiling on held-out projects fails the claim.", "future_outcome_status": "sealed_not_searched"
reports/historical_source_screen_round2.json:23:      "versioned_id": "1910.05923v1", "as_of": "2019-12-31T23:59:59Z", "source_pdf_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1910.05923v1", "source_pdf_sha256": "4aadbe617828bff5de35d92592840540a656c00e149eb625840fa83cfdd7be3f", "source_status": "section_screened", "question_status": "eligible_source_open", "source_locator": "PDF p. 9, Discussion/Grammar Constraints and Conclusion", "source_basis": "The authors report that generated code is often not parseable and propose incorporating grammar rules in future work; the reported dual-task experiments do not test grammar-constrained decoding.", "candidate_question": "With matched training data and compute, do grammar constraints improve parseable and test-passing code generation over this dual-task model without reducing task correctness?", "competing_explanations": "Grammar constraints prevent invalid output; or they trade syntactic validity for semantic correctness and add search cost.", "candidate_falsification": "No held-out task-correctness or test-passing benefit at matched budget, despite higher parseability, fails the substantive claim.", "future_outcome_status": "sealed_not_searched"
reports/historical_source_screen_round2.json:26:      "versioned_id": "1912.01990v1", "as_of": "2019-12-31T23:59:59Z", "source_pdf_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1912.01990v1", "source_pdf_sha256": "8411a850491fa32bbd2fd9f9161310205915e23da35f88bbf4d3aa06b4a80f8d", "source_status": "section_screened", "question_status": "eligible_source_open", "source_locator": "PDF p. 28, §5 Conclusion", "source_basis": "After proving a treewidth-parameterized Hamiltonian Index algorithm, the authors explicitly ask for a faster base or matching lower bound and compare with Hamiltonicity algorithms; they do not resolve this gap.", "candidate_question": "Can Hamiltonian Index be computed with a strictly improved treewidth-exponential base under the paper's graph and decomposition assumptions?", "competing_explanations": "A better dynamic program or algebraic method exists; or a conditional lower bound rules out an improved base.", "candidate_falsification": "A matching conditional lower bound for the specified model/base opposes the faster-algorithm branch; a correct faster algorithm supports it. Runtime experiments alone are insufficient.", "future_outcome_status": "sealed_not_searched"
reports/historical_source_screen_round2.json:32:      "versioned_id": "2004.11573v1", "as_of": "2020-12-31T23:59:59Z", "source_pdf_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2004.11573v1", "source_pdf_sha256": "5bf733d9dc5e0b303c7f0c2dad338add604469ec239b8c0a4b0addfbb3cb5483", "source_status": "section_screened", "question_status": "eligible_source_open", "source_locator": "PDF p. 11, §8 Conclusion and Future Work", "source_basis": "The study characterizes uncommon-uncertainty adversarial examples and generates them; it explicitly leaves using those examples to enhance robustness for future work. Evasion is not evidence of successful training.", "candidate_question": "At equal training-example count and compute, does training on uncommon-uncertainty adversarial examples improve held-out robust accuracy over conventional adversarial training without materially degrading clean accuracy?", "competing_explanations": "Uncommon examples cover missed failures; or they overfit their generator and harm clean performance.", "candidate_falsification": "No held-out robustness advantage, or prespecified clean-accuracy degradation, fails the proposed benefit.", "future_outcome_status": "sealed_not_searched"
reports/historical_source_screen_round2.json:38:      "versioned_id": "2010.14077v1", "as_of": "2020-12-31T23:59:59Z", "source_pdf_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2010.14077v1", "source_pdf_sha256": "f3976c27856ffaf7b89ff6e252da23df1dd190120c9a0217af863cf31140ad55", "source_status": "section_screened", "question_status": "eligible_source_open", "source_locator": "PDF p. 22, §4 Conclusion", "source_basis": "The authors present a lattice-based identity-based encryption with equality testing and flexible authorization, then explicitly leave CCA2 security and stronger models to future work.", "candidate_question": "Can a lattice-based IBE equality-test scheme retain flexible authorization while meeting a precisely specified CCA2 security definition?", "competing_explanations": "A compatible construction and reduction exist; or the authorization/equality interface exposes an unavoidable attack under the proposed definition.", "candidate_falsification": "A valid attack under the claimed CCA2 game defeats a proposed construction; a complete reduction for a defined construction establishes the positive branch only under its stated assumptions.", "future_outcome_status": "sealed_not_searched"
reports/historical_source_screen_round2.json:41:      "versioned_id": "2103.05704v1", "as_of": "2021-12-31T23:59:59Z", "source_pdf_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2103.05704v1", "source_pdf_sha256": "c02e789d6b59b04543710f2e7bf1e621ad05dc3ab5d9025c90a75c9203765e73", "source_status": "section_screened", "question_status": "eligible_source_open", "source_locator": "PDF pp. 15-16, Threats to Validity and Conclusion", "source_basis": "The authors explicitly identify single-context/small preliminary evaluation limits and propose a larger evaluation. The paper's rough time comparison is not a controlled causal estimate.", "candidate_question": "Across independently recruited end-user developers and matched app tasks, does sketch-to-App-Inventor generation improve UI fidelity and completion time relative to manual implementation?", "competing_explanations": "Sketch generation transfers beyond the initial context; or task/developer selection explains the reported benefit.", "candidate_falsification": "No prespecified fidelity or time advantage in an independent matched-task evaluation fails the generalization claim.", "future_outcome_status": "sealed_not_searched"
reports/historical_source_screen_round2.json:50:      "versioned_id": "2106.00714v1", "as_of": "2021-12-31T23:59:59Z", "source_pdf_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2106.00714v1", "source_pdf_sha256": "90db0f7c0c79bee9d0adc23a4a722e3aa50530d3af8c3efe11a15d813a7cb321", "source_status": "section_screened", "question_status": "eligible_source_open", "source_locator": "PDF p. 20, §7 Conclusion", "source_basis": "The paper gives results for polynomial permanents and shortest disjoint cycles, then explicitly asks whether its framework can extend to shortest k disjoint cycles for k ≥ 3. Its existing result does not answer the k=3 case.", "candidate_question": "Can the paper's algebraic framework be extended to compute shortest three disjoint cycles in its weighted-graph setting with a proved parallel bound?", "competing_explanations": "The algebra extends to three cycles; or cancellations/state growth prevent the claimed bound.", "candidate_falsification": "A correct counterexample to a proposed three-cycle extension defeats that extension; a fully proved algorithm with the specified assumptions and bound supports it. Related two-cycle results do not settle it.", "future_outcome_status": "sealed_not_searched"
reports/historical_source_screen_round2.json:53:      "versioned_id": "2109.08630v1", "as_of": "2021-12-31T23:59:59Z", "source_pdf_url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/2109.08630v1", "source_pdf_sha256": "25bc47a635f22330b8a54bf5cdc74f4b6ff3b950719f02526950b9a249b087fe", "source_status": "section_screened", "question_status": "eligible_source_open", "source_locator": "PDF pp. 7, 11, future-work discussion and §9 Discussion", "source_basis": "The fairness analysis studies its stated PATE setup; it explicitly notes that alternative GNMAX/interactive voting and teacher-ensemble-size choices may change group fairness, which it does not evaluate.", "candidate_question": "At matched privacy budget and data, does interactive GNMAX voting produce a different between-group excessive-risk disparity than noninteractive PATE aggregation because of selection effects?", "competing_explanations": "Interactive selection changes representation and group risk; or its effect disappears after matching privacy, sample and ensemble size.", "candidate_falsification": "No reproducible group-disparity difference across independent datasets with matched privacy and selection accounting fails the claimed effect.", "future_outcome_status": "sealed_not_searched"
